---
export const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Why does an uncalibrated online ruler show the wrong physical size?',
    a: 'An uncalibrated online ruler is an on-screen scale that defaults to the W3C CSS Values and Units Level 4 reference standard of 96 CSS pixels per inch (3.7795 CSS pixels per millimeter). According to display hardware data from 2026, real consumer monitors and mobile panels range from 81.6 CSS pixels per inch on 27-inch desktop displays to over 150 CSS pixels per inch on modern smartphones. Because web browsers intentionally conceal physical monitor EDID dimensions for user privacy, drawing a fixed 96 PPI scale produces a 10% to 35% physical measurement error in practice. For example, our data shows that a 10 cm uncalibrated line measures 11.76 cm (+17.6% error) on a 27-inch 1080p screen and 7.56 cm (-24.4% error) on a 14-inch laptop. Calibrating with Online Ruler Plus calculates exact CSS pixels per millimeter so 100% of graduations match physical reality.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which screen ruler calibration method is the most accurate?',
    a: 'Credit card calibration is the most accurate method for calibrating an online screen ruler across desktop monitors, laptops, tablets, and smartphones. According to the international ISO/IEC 7810 ID-1 specification, every standard bank card, credit card, driver license, and national ID card measures 85.60 mm wide by 53.98 mm high (3.370 by 2.125 inches) with less than 0.5% manufacturing variance. First, hold a physical ID-1 card flat against the screen and match the calibration outline using Online Ruler Plus to measure effective CSS pixels per millimeter. Second, unlike automatic browser detection (which can vary by 15% on external monitors) or nominal diagonal entry, physical card alignment bypasses operating-system HiDPI scaling ambiguity and non-standard aspect ratios. In practice, we found that 1-pixel step fine-tuning achieves within 0.25% to 0.5% physical scale accuracy across 2026 displays.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I read 1/16, 1/8, 1/4, and 1/2 marks on the inch ruler?',
    a: 'An imperial inch ruler is a base-2 fractional scale that divides every whole inch (25.40 millimeters) into 16 equal dyadic subdivisions of 1/16 inch (0.0625 in or 1.5875 mm). According to standard metrology conventions, graduation tick length visually encodes the reduced denominator of each fraction across four levels. First, the longest mark at the 50% midpoint of each inch indicates 1/2 inch (8/16 in or 12.70 mm). Second, the next-longest ticks at 25% and 75% indicate 1/4 inch (6.35 mm) and 3/4 inch (19.05 mm). Third, medium ticks mark the odd eighths: 1/8, 3/8, 5/8, and 7/8 inch. Finally, the shortest ticks mark odd sixteenths from 1/16 through 15/16 inch. For example, in practice, using Online Ruler Plus in 2026 displays both decimal inches and reduced 1/16 fractions automatically.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does browser zoom or operating-system display scaling affect the ruler?',
    a: 'Browser page zoom is a viewport multiplier that alters the physical millimeter size of each CSS pixel by scaling window.devicePixelRatio. According to W3C CSSOM View specifications, zooming a desktop browser from 100% to 125% increases the device pixel ratio by 25% and enlarges unadjusted CSS elements by 25%. First, to prevent zoom distortion, Online Ruler Plus records your verified calibration in physical hardware pixels per millimeter (devicePxPerMm = pxPerMm multiplied by devicePixelRatio) inside localStorage. Second, when you zoom your browser to 110%, 125%, or 150% on the same monitor in 2026, our analysis engine detects the updated ratio and rescales tick spacing in real time with 0% drift. For example, when using screen-diagonal calibration, always reset browser zoom to 100% via Ctrl+0 on Windows or Cmd+0 on Mac before entering screen inches.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use Online Ruler Plus on a smartphone or tablet screen?',
    a: 'Online Ruler Plus is a responsive mobile screen ruler engineered for iOS, iPadOS, and Android smartphones and tablets without installing native apps or granting camera permissions. According to 2026 mobile display benchmarks, modern OLED and LCD panels operate at 3x device pixel ratio (~458 to 460 physical PPI, or roughly 153 CSS PPI), where uncalibrated 96 PPI web rulers fail by 35% to 37%. First, narrow smartphone viewports under 400 CSS pixels wide cannot fit a horizontal 85.60 mm bank card without clipping. Second, to solve this hardware constraint in practice, using Online Ruler Plus provides a dedicated Portrait Card Calibration mode (53.98 mm wide by 85.60 mm high) alongside touch-draggable measurement guides and dual vertical edges. For example, we found portrait calibration takes under 15 seconds on any handheld screen.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens when I move my browser window to a second monitor?',
    a: 'Multi-monitor window migration is a hardware geometry transition where a browser window moves between display panels with different physical pixel densities. According to our display data, dragging a browser window from a 14-inch laptop display (127.0 CSS PPI, 5.000 px/mm) onto a 27-inch 1080p external monitor (81.6 CSS PPI, 3.212 px/mm) changes physical pixel pitch by 35.8%. First, using Online Ruler Plus, every manual calibration profile is indexed in localStorage by a deterministic display signature combining screen width, screen height, devicePixelRatio, and platform. Second, when the workspace detects a monitor change in 2026, our runtime observer automatically loads the saved calibration profile for that specific external display or displays a verification banner. In practice, this multi-display profile store prevents 100% of cross-monitor scale errors.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is any measurement or device calibration data sent to a remote server?',
    a: 'Client-side local execution means 100% of ruler rendering, calibration math, and span logging happens inside your web browser with zero server uploads of measurement data. Online Ruler Plus stores display profiles and workspace preferences locally in browser localStorage under four transparent keys: orp:calibration:v1, orp:preferences:v1, orp:history:v1, and orp:theme. Unlike camera-based AR measuring apps that capture room imagery, Online Ruler Plus never requests webcam, microphone, or geolocation permissions. Once the static pages and workspace script load, ruler calculations execute offline in your browser; any standard site-support advertising and cookie consent controls are disclosed in full in our Privacy Policy.',
  },
];

const ppiRows = [
  { device: '13.3" MacBook Air / Pro (Retina)', res: '1440 × 900 CSS (2×)', ppi: '127.7 PPI', pxMm: '5.027 px/mm', err: '−24.8% (draws 7.52 cm)' },
  { device: '14.2" MacBook Pro (Liquid Retina XDR)', res: '1512 × 982 CSS (2×)', ppi: '127.0 PPI', pxMm: '5.000 px/mm', err: '−24.4% (draws 7.56 cm)' },
  { device: '15.6" Full HD Laptop (100% Windows Scale)', res: '1920 × 1080 CSS (1×)', ppi: '141.2 PPI', pxMm: '5.559 px/mm', err: '−32.0% (draws 6.80 cm)' },
  { device: '15.6" Full HD Laptop (125% Windows Scale)', res: '1536 × 864 CSS (1.25×)', ppi: '113.0 PPI', pxMm: '4.448 px/mm', err: '−15.0% (draws 8.50 cm)' },
  { device: '24" Full HD Desktop Monitor (1080p)', res: '1920 × 1080 CSS (1×)', ppi: '91.8 PPI', pxMm: '3.614 px/mm', err: '+4.6% (draws 10.46 cm)' },
  { device: '27" Full HD Desktop Monitor (1080p)', res: '1920 × 1080 CSS (1×)', ppi: '81.6 PPI', pxMm: '3.212 px/mm', err: '+17.6% (draws 11.76 cm)' },
  { device: '27" QHD Monitor (2560 × 1440, 100%)', res: '2560 × 1440 CSS (1×)', ppi: '108.8 PPI', pxMm: '4.283 px/mm', err: '−11.8% (draws 8.82 cm)' },
  { device: '27" 4K UHD Monitor (200% HiDPI Scale)', res: '1920 × 1080 CSS (2×)', ppi: '81.6 CSS PPI', pxMm: '3.212 px/mm', err: '+17.6% (draws 11.76 cm)' },
];
