<!-- SECTION 2: ACCURACY & PPI SCIENCE (Dark Charcoal Surface Tile #272729 per DESIGN.md) -->
<section
  id="accuracy-guide"
  aria-labelledby="accuracy-heading"
  class="apple-tile-dark"
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1440px] px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 lg:flex-row lg:items-end lg:justify-between">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-primary-on-dark">
          Display Optics · W3C CSS Reference Pixel vs. Hardware PPI
        </p>
        <h2 id="accuracy-heading" class="mt-1.5 type-display-lg text-on-dark">
          Why do online screen rulers require PPI calibration?
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl type-body text-body-muted">
          Physical pixel density (Pixels Per Inch, or PPI) is determined by a display panel’s physical millimeter width and native pixel resolution, whereas web browsers render layouts using abstract 1/96-inch CSS reference pixels.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="shrink-0">
        <a href="/calibration-guide" class="apple-btn-primary">
          Complete Calibration Guide
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <!-- Left 5 Columns: Technical Explanations -->
      <div class="space-y-6 lg:col-span-5">
        <div class="rounded-[18px] border border-white/12 bg-surface-tile-2 p-6">
          <h3 class="type-tagline text-on-dark">
            Why does 96 CSS pixels rarely equal one physical inch?
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[65ch] type-caption text-body-muted">
            A CSS reference pixel is defined by the W3C CSS Values and Units Level 4 standard as exactly 1/96 of an inch (0.2646 mm, or 3.7795 CSS pixels per millimeter) for arm’s-length viewing consistency. However, physical monitor panels manufactured in 2026 range from 81.6 PPI on 27-inch 1080p desktops to 254 physical PPI (127 CSS PPI at 2x Retina scaling) on 14-inch laptops. Without calibration, any web ruler assuming 96 PPI miscalculates physical lengths by 10% to 35%. Online Ruler Plus overrides the 96 PPI fallback with your display’s measured CSS pixels per millimeter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-[18px] border border-white/12 bg-surface-tile-2 p-6">
          <h3 class="type-tagline text-on-dark">
            What are standard reference object dimensions for verification?
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[65ch] type-caption text-body-muted">
            Standard reference objects are internationally regulated physical items with tight manufacturing tolerances used to verify screen ruler calibration. According to ISO/IEC 7810:2019, an ID-1 bank or credit card measures 85.60 × 53.98 mm (3.370 × 2.125 inches). According to United States Mint and European Central Bank specifications, a US Quarter measures 24.26 mm (0.955 in), a US Penny measures 19.05 mm (3/4 in), a US Nickel measures 21.21 mm, and a 1 Euro coin measures 23.25 mm. Holding any of these items against the calibrated scale confirms 1:1 accuracy immediately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-[18px] border border-white/12 bg-surface-tile-2 p-6">
          <h3 class="type-tagline text-on-dark">
            What are the physical limitations of on-screen measurement?
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[65ch] type-caption text-body-muted">
            On-screen metrology is subject to three physical hardware boundaries: active panel span, cover-glass parallax, and sub-pixel rounding. First, maximum single-pass measurement length is bounded by your visible monitor width (typically 31 cm on a 14-inch laptop or 59.8 cm on a 27-inch display). Second, thick monitor cover glass introduces optical parallax if viewed at an oblique angle; always keep your line of sight perpendicular to the tick mark. Third, screen rulers are designed for everyday, craft, and UI reference—not safety-critical machining where vernier calipers are required.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Right 7 Columns: Reference PPI Table -->
      <div class="rounded-[18px] border border-white/12 bg-surface-tile-2 p-6 lg:col-span-7 flex flex-col justify-between">
        <div>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2">
            <div>
              <span class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-primary-on-dark">
                Hardware Density Benchmark Table (2026)
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-1 type-tagline text-on-dark">
                Common Display Panels &amp; Uncalibrated 96 PPI Error on a 10 cm Object
              </h3>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/15 font-mono text-[11px] text-body-muted">
                  <th class="py-2.5 pr-3 font-semibold">Display Panel / Scaling</th>
                  <th class="py-2.5 px-3 font-semibold">CSS Viewport</th>
                  <th class="py-2.5 px-3 font-semibold">Effective CSS PPI</th>
                  <th class="py-2.5 px-3 font-semibold">CSS px/mm</th>
                  <th class="py-2.5 pl-3 font-semibold">Uncalibrated 96 PPI Error</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 font-mono text-xs">
                {
                  ppiRows.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td class="py-3 pr-3 font-sans font-medium text-on-dark">{row.device}</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-3 text-body-muted">{row.res}</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-3 font-semibold text-primary-on-dark">{row.ppi}</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-3 text-on-dark">{row.pxMm}</td>
                      <td class="py-3 pl-3 text-body-muted">{row.err}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-5 border-t border-white/12 pt-4 type-fine-print text-body-muted">
          Formula: <code class="font-mono text-on-dark">Effective CSS PPI = √(W_css² + H_css²) / diagonalInches</code> · <code class="font-mono text-on-dark">pxPerMm = CSS_PPI / 25.4</code>. Under the international yard and pound agreement (NIST / BIPM), 1 inch equals 25.4 millimeters exactly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

